(3,'🟡',"Design tracing for a multi-step LLM request (retrieval, rerank, LLM call, tool call, retry): what is a trace vs a span, what attributes go on an LLM span (model, token counts, finish reason, prompt version), and how do OpenTelemetry's GenAI semantic conventions and vendors such as Langfuse, LangSmith and Phoenix relate? (Part 8 Q38 covers agent step traces.)",f"all types; platform teams, GenAI startups; Langfuse, LangSmith and Phoenix docs describe this; OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions have moved repos and are still evolving"),
(3,'🟡',"What do you monitor in production for an LLM feature, and which alerts would page someone: error and timeout rate, latency percentiles, token and cost per request, refusal and guardrail-trigger rate, format-failure rate, user thumbs-down, and sampled quality scores? How do you choose thresholds? (Part 9 Q30 has online A/B and judging.)",f"all types; product companies, GCCs, platform teams; {G}"),
(3,'🟡',"Quality, input and output drift in an LLM application: what can change without a code change (provider model update, user query mix, retrieved corpus, prompt template), how do you detect each (embedding or length distributions, rolling judge scores on sampled traffic, statistical tests), and what are the limits of 'hallucination rate' proxies?",f"all types; GenAI-engineer, MLE rounds; {G} (MLflow checklist lists drift from provider updates, input shifts and retrieval changes)"),
(3,'🟡',"How do you manage prompts in production: prompts as code in the repo vs a prompt registry or config store (versions, labels such as staging and production, environments, diffs, audit trail, client-side caching), who can change them, and how do you link each trace to a prompt version? (Part 3 Q33 has the short version.)",f"all types; product companies, GCCs, GenAI startups; Langfuse prompt-management docs describe versions, labels and caching"),
(3,'🟡',"Roll out a prompt or model change safely: offline eval gate, shadow traffic, canary percentage, A/B, feature flag, automatic rollback triggers, and what to compare. How do you roll back when the 'deployment' is a label move rather than a code deploy? (Part 9 Q14 owns the CI gate; Part 9 Q43 covers provider updates.)",f"all types; product companies, platform teams; {G}"),
(3,'🟡',"Prompt injection: direct vs indirect, why it is called the top LLM risk (the model cannot reliably separate instructions from data), and a realistic attack chain against an assistant that reads email or web pages. (Part 3 Q34 and Part 6 Q36 are the intro; this goes to attack chains and the OWASP framing.)",f"all types; security-aware GenAI roles, product companies, GCCs; {O} lists it as LLM01; Greshake et al. (2023) is the primary paper"),
(3,'🟡',"Walk through the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025 edition: prompt injection, sensitive information disclosure, supply chain, data and model poisoning, improper output handling, excessive agency, system prompt leakage, vector and embedding weaknesses, misinformation, unbounded consumption). Pick the four that matter most for a RAG chatbot with tools and defend the choice.",f"all types; security-aware GenAI roles; opened from the OWASP GenAI site; {G} style question"),
(3,'🟡',"What is the 'lethal trifecta' (access to private data, exposure to untrusted content, ability to communicate externally)? How do you use it to review an agent design, and why does breaking one leg beat adding a smarter filter?",f"senior-leaning GenAI roles, security-aware teams; Simon Willison's post defines it; {G} style question"),
(3,'🟡',"Defence in depth against prompt injection: input screening, structured delimiting of untrusted content, instruction hierarchy, output filtering, least-privilege tools, human approval for risky actions, sandboxing and monitoring. Which layers are probabilistic and which are deterministic, and what is the honest residual risk? (Part 5 Q8 and Q9 cover guardrail layers; Part 8 Q40 agent guardrails.)",f"all types; product companies, GCCs, security-aware roles; Anthropic and OpenAI guidance list these layers"),
